Since you're learning CI/CD without AWS or Azure, this is the correct flow:
1     Git
2      ↓
3     GitHub
4      ↓
5     GitHub Actions
6      ↓
7     Docker
8      ↓
9     Ansible
10      ↓
11     Kind Kubernetes
1. Git (Version Control)
Install
Ubuntu/Codespaces:
1     sudo apt update
2     sudo apt install git -y
Verify
1     git --version
Configure
1     git config --global user.name "Your Name"
2     git config --global user.email "your@email.com"
Basic Workflow
Create project:
1     mkdir demo-app
2     cd demo-app
3     git init
Add files:
1     git add .
2     git commit -m "Initial Commit"

2. GitHub (Repository)
Create a repository on GitHub.
Connect local Git to GitHub:
1     git remote add origin https://github.com/USERNAME/demo-app.git
Push code:
1     git push -u origin main
Check:
1     git remote -v

3. GitHub Actions (CI/CD)
Create:
1     .github/
2     └── workflows/
3         └── ci-cd.yml
Example:
1     name: CI Pipeline
2     
3     on:
4       push:
5         branches:
6           - main
7     
8     jobs:
9       build:
10         runs-on: ubuntu-latest
11     
12         steps:
13         - uses: actions/checkout@v4
14     
15         - name: Build Docker Image
16           run: docker build -t demo-app .
How It Works
1.	Push code to GitHub
2.	GitHub Action starts automatically
3.	Builds application
4.	Builds Docker image
5.	Runs tests
6.	Deploys

4. Docker
Install
1     sudo apt update
2     sudo apt install docker.io -y
3     
4     sudo systemctl start docker
5     sudo systemctl enable docker
If using Codespaces:
1     docker --version
Test
1     docker run hello-world
Create Dockerfile
1     FROM nginx
2     
3     COPY . /usr/share/nginx/html
Build:
1     docker build -t myapp:v1 .
Run:
1     docker run -d -p 8080:80 myapp:v1
Check:
1     docker ps

5. Ansible
Install
1     sudo apt update
2     sudo apt install ansible -y
Verify:
1     ansible --version
Inventory:
1     [kubernetes]
2     localhost ansible_connection=local
Test:
1     ansible all -i inventory.ini -m ping
Playbook Example
1     ---
2     - name: Deploy Application
3       hosts: kubernetes
4     
5       tasks:
6         - name: Display Message
7           debug:
8             msg: "Deployment Started"
Run:
1     ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini deploy.yml

6. Kind Kubernetes
Kind = Kubernetes IN Docker
Install Kind
1     curl -Lo ./kind https://kind.sigs.k8s.io/dl/latest/kind-linux-amd64
2     
3     chmod +x kind
4     
5     sudo mv kind /usr/local/bin/
Verify:
1     kind version
Create Cluster
1     kind create cluster --name lab
Check:
1     kubectl get nodes
You should see:
1     NAME                 STATUS
2     lab-control-plane    Ready

Install kubectl
1     sudo snap install kubectl --classic
Verify:
1     kubectl version --client

Deploy Application to Kind
Create deployment:
1     apiVersion: apps/v1
2     kind: Deployment
3     metadata:
4       name: webapp
5     
6     spec:
7       replicas: 2
8     
9       selector:
10         matchLabels:
11           app: webapp
12     
13       template:
14         metadata:
15           labels:
16             app: webapp
17     
18         spec:
19           containers:
20           - name: webapp
21             image: nginx
Apply:
1     kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
Check:
1     kubectl get pods

Complete CI/CD Flow
1     Developer
2        │
3        ▼
4     Git Commit
5        │
6        ▼
7     GitHub Push
8        │
9        ▼
10     GitHub Actions
11     (Build + Test)
12        │
13        ▼
14     Docker Build
15        │
16        ▼
17     Docker Image
18        │
19        ▼
20     Ansible Playbook
21        │
22        ▼
23     Kind Kubernetes
24        │
25        ▼
26     Application Running
For your GitHub Codespaces lab
Install in this order:
1     1. Git
2     2. Docker
3     3. kubectl
4     4. Kind
5     5. Ansible
6     6. GitHub Repository
7     7. GitHub Actions
This gives you a 100% free local/browser-based DevOps lab using Git + GitHub + GitHub Actions + Docker + Ansible + Kubernetes (Kind) without creating any AWS or Azure resources.
